From: MyWirelessAndroidAutoDongle contributors
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: prepare AIC Bluetooth reference pin defaults

The working V1.12M/AIC8800D80 reference keeps GPIO0 PC1 output low,
PB3 input low and PB4 input high while Bluetooth is powered and the vendor
rfkill instance is blocked. Its rfkill probe drives PC1 low, but the later
power-on and RTS pulse path is not used. AIC firmware loading enables BT
with UART low-power mode disabled; do not invent a GPIO reset pulse.

Hold PC1 physically low with pull-down and leave PB3/PB4 as pulled-up inputs.
Match the reference Schmitt settings and raw drive register fields: the
RK3566 setter accepts levels, not the generic diagnostic mA labels. Level 0
encodes 0x01 for the three GPIOs, level 3 encodes 0x0f for UART RX/TX/RTS,
and level 1 encodes 0x03 for CTS. Do not copy the diagnostic 4/12 labels.

Input hogs reserve the lines and do not provide wake IRQ handling: revisit
ownership before enabling GPIO wake or btlpm. UART1 retains all four flow
control pins. Wi-Fi PC0 remains owned by mmc-pwrseq-simple; the generic ZERO
3W tree stays unchanged. Slew rate is not set by the mainline driver.

This is a compiled experiment candidate, not evidence of mainline operation.
Apply after the board's 0004 AIC DT preparation patch.

Sources:
https://github.com/radxa/kernel/blob/fd366906451692033f1ba273e77e7e2f936c78db/net/rfkill/rfkill-bt.c
https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/85d82e0d7c2dfd3e75d34f35b7ebbf41f45c9c4e/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c
https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/85d82e0d7c2dfd3e75d34f35b7ebbf41f45c9c4e/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/gpio.txt
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800d80.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800d80.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800d80.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800d80.dts
@@ -16,7 +16,79 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&gpio0 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&aic_bt_pc1 &aic_bt_pb3 &aic_bt_pb4>;
+
+	/* Preserve reference directions; no reset pulse is implied. */
+	aic-bt-pc1-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <RK_PC1 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		output-low;
+		line-name = "aic-bt-pc1-low";
+	};
+
+	aic-bt-pb3-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <RK_PB3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		input;
+		line-name = "aic-bt-pb3-input";
+	};
+
+	aic-bt-pb4-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <RK_PB4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		input;
+		line-name = "aic-bt-pb4-input";
+	};
+};
+
 &pinctrl {
+	/* RK3566 drive-strength values are levels, not diagnostic mA labels. */
+	aic_bt_pulldown: pcfg-aic-bt-pulldown {
+		bias-pull-down;
+		drive-strength = <0>;
+		input-schmitt-enable;
+	};
+
+	aic_bt_pullup: pcfg-aic-bt-pullup {
+		bias-pull-up;
+		drive-strength = <0>;
+		input-schmitt-enable;
+	};
+
+	aic_uart_pullup: pcfg-aic-uart-pullup {
+		bias-pull-up;
+		drive-strength = <3>;
+		input-schmitt-enable;
+	};
+
+	aic_uart_rts: pcfg-aic-uart-rts {
+		bias-disable;
+		drive-strength = <3>;
+		input-schmitt-enable;
+	};
+
+	aic_uart_cts: pcfg-aic-uart-cts {
+		bias-disable;
+		drive-strength = <1>;
+		input-schmitt-enable;
+	};
+
+	bluetooth {
+		aic_bt_pc1: aic-bt-pc1 {
+			rockchip,pins = <0 RK_PC1 RK_FUNC_GPIO &aic_bt_pulldown>;
+		};
+
+		aic_bt_pb3: aic-bt-pb3 {
+			rockchip,pins = <0 RK_PB3 RK_FUNC_GPIO &aic_bt_pullup>;
+		};
+
+		aic_bt_pb4: aic-bt-pb4 {
+			rockchip,pins = <0 RK_PB4 RK_FUNC_GPIO &aic_bt_pullup>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	antenna {
 		aic_antenna_enable: antenna-enable {
 			rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PD3 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_down>;
@@ -52,3 +124,16 @@
 &sdmmc1_cmd {
 	rockchip,pins = <2 RK_PA7 1 &pcfg_pull_up_drv_level_12>;
 };
+
+&uart1m0_ctsn {
+	rockchip,pins = <2 RK_PB6 2 &aic_uart_cts>;
+};
+
+&uart1m0_rtsn {
+	rockchip,pins = <2 RK_PB5 2 &aic_uart_rts>;
+};
+
+&uart1m0_xfer {
+	rockchip,pins = <2 RK_PB3 2 &aic_uart_pullup>,
+		       <2 RK_PB4 2 &aic_uart_pullup>;
+};
